The most efficient way to sort integer numbers up to 1000 is to use a counting sort algorithm. Counting sort is a comparison-free sorting algorithm that works by counting the number of objects having distinct key values. It is particularly efficient for sorting integers in a specific range.

Here's a step-by-step explanation of the counting sort algorithm:

1.  Determine the range of the input values, which in this case is 0 to 1000.
2.  Create an array of size 1001 to store the count of each number from 0 to 1000.
3.  Initialize all elements of the count array to 0.
4.  Iterate over the input array and for each number, increment the corresponding count in the count array.
5.  Create another array to store the sorted numbers.
6.  Iterate over the count array and copy the numbers from the input array to the sorted array based on the count.
7.  Return the sorted array.

The time complexity of counting sort is O(n + k), where n is the number of elements and k is the range of the input values. In this case, n is 1000 and k is also 1000, so the time complexity is O(1000 + 1000) = O(2000).

Here's a sample implementation in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void counting_sort(int arr[], int n) {
    int count[1001] = {0};
    int sorted[1000];

    // Count the occurrences of each number
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        count[arr[i]]++;
    }

    // Copy the numbers from the count array to the sorted array
    int j = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 1001; i++) {
        while (count[i] > 0) {
            sorted[j++] = i;
            count[i]--;
        }
    }

    // Copy the sorted array to the original array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        arr[i] = sorted[i];
    }
}

void print_array(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {4, 2, 2, 8, 3, 3, 1};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    printf("Original array: ");
    print_array(arr, n);

    counting_sort(arr, n);

    printf("Sorted array: ");
    print_array(arr, n);

    return 0;
}
```

This implementation sorts the array in ascending order. The time complexity is O(2000), making it efficient for sorting integers up to 1000.

